    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1931, economic hardship led to Min Buri Province dissolving, and Nong Chok was transferred to Chachoengsao Province.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nong_Chok_district"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The district’s original settlers were Muslims relocated from southern Thailand, giving Nong Chok a distinct cultural heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nong_Chok_district"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rice, vegetables, fruits, and livestock remain the backbone of Nong Chok’s economy, with farms still thriving today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nong_Chok_district"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Nong Chok hosts the vast Nong Chok Wetland, a critical habitat for migratory birds and a growing eco‑tourism spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
